  "heat": 1,
  "text": "She was a widow for eighty-four years. She spent all of it inside one building. Waiting. Luke names her. Anna. A prophetess. \"The daughter of Phanuel, of the tribe of Aser.\" Stop on that. Asher. One of the northern tribes Assyria scattered off the map centuries earlier. The woman standing in the temple is from a people most of the country had written off. Married seven years. Then her husband died. And Luke says she was \"a widow of about fourscore and four years.\" Quick honesty note, because people fudge this. Translators genuinely split. Either she was eighty-four, or widowed for eighty-four years. Anybody picking whichever version preaches better is doing you dirty. Either way. Decades. \"Which departed not from the temple, but served God with fastings and prayers night and day.\" Night and day. In a building. Waiting on something she had no proof was ever coming. Then one ordinary morning a poor couple walks in with a newborn to do what the law required. Nothing about it looks like anything. \"And she coming in that instant.\" That instant. And then she does the thing nobody else in the scene does. Simeon blesses the parents and speaks to the family. Anna walks out and tells the city. \"Spake of him to all them that looked for redemption in Jerusalem.\" Eighty-four years of nothing. And she got the scoop.",
